     6	
     7	# /night-mode — Owner autonomy posture toggle (PLAN-165)
     8	
     9	Usage: `/night-mode on`, `/night-mode off`, or `/night-mode status`.
    10	
    11	Arms or disarms a per-machine autonomy posture: `on` sets
    12	`permissions.defaultMode: "acceptEdits"` in the gitignored
    13	`.claude/settings.local.json` overlay; `off` restores the snapshotted
    14	prior value. The tracked `.claude/settings.json` (the published
    15	fail-closed default, `defaultMode: "manual"`) is **never touched**. All
    16	logic lives in `.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`.
    17	
    18	## ⛔ This command does NOT run the toggle — you do
    19	
    20	**Owner-ratified OQ1-redo (2026-08-03): arming or disarming the autonomy
    21	posture is a HUMAN action.** The model rail does not flip the posture of
    22	your next session; a human at the keyboard does.
    23	
    24	**What actually enforces this, without overclaiming** (codex S292 r7 P1 —
    25	the earlier wording said "denies every model-rail invocation", which is
    26	false and measurably so):
    27	
    28	- **Closed, both rails:** Edit/Write of the writer, the overlay and the
    29	  marker (`.claude/settings.json` deny + `_CANONICAL_GUARDS`, which the
    30	  Bash rail keys off). A tool write or a shell redirect cannot touch them.
    31	- **Best-effort, not a boundary:** `check_bash_safety`
    32	  (`_e4_check_posture_toggle_invocation`, PLAN-165 NF-08) blocks the
    33	  invocation shapes a STATIC shell parse can recognise — and static
    34	  parsing of bash is not complete. A RENAMED INTERPRETER
    35	  (`./zz-interp .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on`) is measured to pass
    36	  both the matcher and the script's self-path guard, because the script
    37	  that ends up running IS the canonical one
    38	  (`nf08-invocation-guard-NOTES.md` §7 residual 5).
    39	- **Therefore:** a session with broad Bash permission can still arm the
    40	  next session's posture. These controls raise the cost and make the
    41	  honest path obvious; they do not make it impossible. The real perimeter
    42	  is the CURRENT session's `defaultMode` + Bash allowlist.
    43	
    44	Reading the script and its state (cat/grep/git) stays open.
    45	
    46	So this command is a **teleprompter, not a driver**. Claude's whole job
    47	here is to hand you one line to run yourself.
    48	
    49	### How Claude must respond
    50	
    51	1. Parse the first token of `$ARGUMENTS` as the subcommand. Accept
    52	   case-insensitive `on` / `off` / `status`. Default (no argument) =
    53	   `status`. Reject any other token with a short usage line — do not
    54	   guess.
    55	2. Print the matching command from the sections below, prefixed with
    56	   `!` so the operator can run it on the human rail of this session
    57	   (the `!` prefix executes in the terminal, not through the model's
    58	   Bash tool). A terminal outside Claude Code works identically.
    59	3. Print the one-line reminder that goes with that subcommand.
    60	4. **Stop.** Do not call Bash. Do not "helpfully" verify by running the
    61	   script. If the operator pastes the output back, read it and explain
    62	   it — that is the whole loop.
    63	
    64	## Next-session semantics (the whole point)
    65	
    66	Settings are read at **session start**. The toggle therefore takes
    67	effect on the **next** session, never the current one:
    68	
    69	> Turn it on before you go to sleep → the overnight session starts in
    70	> `acceptEdits` → turn it off in the morning.
    71	
    72	The currently running governed session never changes posture under the
    73	operator. If you need autonomy *now*, start a new session after `on`.
    74	
    75	## `on` — arm night mode
    76	
    77	Command for the operator to run:
    78	
    79	```
    80	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on
    81	```
    82	
    83	Reminder to print with it:
    84	`night-mode: takes effect on the NEXT session; run /night-mode off in the morning.`
    85	
    86	The script (single writer — direct Edit/Write of the overlay is denied
    87	by policy):
    88	
    89	1. Merge-writes `permissions.defaultMode: "acceptEdits"` into
    90	   `.claude/settings.local.json` (lock → atomic temp+`os.replace` →
    91	   read-back; malformed existing JSON is fail-CLOSED: non-zero exit,
    92	   file untouched).
    93	2. Snapshots the prior value **create-only** into the marker so a
    94	   double `on` can never make the weak posture the thing `off`
    95	   "restores".
    96	3. Writes the marker+snapshot file `.claude/state/night-mode.json`
    97	   (gitignored via `.claude/state/`) with timestamp, hostname, and the
    98	   mode it wrote.
    99	4. Emits the `night_mode_toggled` audit action (HMAC-chained).
   100	
   101	`on` twice is an idempotent no-op (exit 0). The script **refuses to run
   102	when `CI` is set** — this is an operator/local-machine control only.
   103	
   104	## `off` — disarm night mode
   105	
   106	```
   107	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py off
   108	```
   109	
   110	Restores the snapshotted `defaultMode` (or removes the key if none
   111	existed), then removes the marker — reverse order of `on`, so a crash
   112	in between is detectable, never silent. `off` twice is an idempotent
   113	no-op (exit 0). Takes effect on the next session.
   114	
   115	The marker is validated as a **whole document** before any of its fields
   116	is acted on (version, `mode_written`, both booleans, `prev_value` against
   117	a closed set derived from the harness's real `--permission-mode` enum,
   118	and the `created_file`/`prev_present` consistency rule). Anything that a
   119	healthy `on` could not have written is fail-CLOSED: exit 2, overlay
   120	untouched, marker left in place as evidence. `bypassPermissions` and
   121	`acceptEdits` are **never** restorable values.
   122	
   123	### `off --discard-snapshot` — recovery from a refusal
   124	
   125	```
   126	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py off --discard-snapshot
   127	```
   128	
   129	The sanctioned exit from **every** fail-closed refusal above: it removes
   130	the local `defaultMode` override **and** the marker *without* honoring
   131	`prev_value`, printing exactly what it discarded. A refusal must never
   132	leave the posture armed with no way to disarm it.
   133	
   134	- It also disarms the **armed-without-marker** state, where plain `off`
   135	  is a no-op (crash between the two writes, or a hand-deleted marker).
   136	- It removes **one key, never a file** — an overlay carrying your other
   137	  settings survives with `defaultMode` stripped.
   138	- It is still fail-CLOSED on a malformed overlay (it must rewrite that
   139	  file; repair the JSON by hand first).
   140	- The next session resolves the project layer's ratified posture. If you
   141	  wanted a specific prior value back, set it yourself after recovering —
   142	  discard deliberately does not trust the snapshot it just threw away.
   143	
   144	## `status` — report resolved posture
   145	
   146	```
   147	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py status
   148	```
   149	
   150	Prints:
   151	
   152	- the **resolved** `permissions.defaultMode` per layer, via
   153	  `_lib/effective_config.resolve_settings()` — the same resolver the
   154	  tamper tripwires use, with layer provenance (which layer won:
   155	  project vs local);
   156	- the marker's age and contents (when armed, by whom/where);
   157	- whether the marker and the resolved configuration **agree**.
   158	
   159	Truth comes from the resolver, not the marker. The marker is
   160	decoration.
   161	
   162	`status` is read-only, and it is denied to the model rail anyway: the
   163	invocation guard keys off the *script*, not the subcommand, because a
   164	permissive carve-out driven by argv parsing is exactly the shape the
   165	fail-closed doctrine rejects. Claude does have a model-rail read of the
   166	same fact — **`/ceo-boot`'s advisory night-mode banner is
   167	resolver-derived** and needs no subprocess. Use that when Claude needs
   168	to *know* the posture; use `! … status` when you want the full report.
   169	
   170	## Escape valve — real bypass is NOT this command
   171	
   172	Night-mode tops out at `acceptEdits`. It never writes
   173	`bypassPermissions` — that value trips the `settings_tamper_tripwires`
   174	red in `/ceo-boot` by design, in any layer (PLAN-165 D1). If you truly
   175	need a full-bypass session, the honest path is one-shot and explicit:
   176	
   177	```
   178	claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions   # one session, explicit, ephemeral
   179	```
   180	
   181	No persistent state, no tripwire collision, nothing for morning-you to
   182	forget. Never route bypass through night-mode.
   183	
   184	## Acceptance invariant — tracked tree stays clean
   185	
   186	After any subcommand, `git status --porcelain` must be empty (AC-1).
   187	Both files the toggle touches are gitignored:
   188	`.claude/settings.local.json` and `.claude/state/night-mode.json`. If
   189	the tree is dirty after a toggle, something wrote outside the contract
   190	— stop and investigate before committing anything.
   191	
   192	## Troubleshooting
   193	
   194	- **"Claude refused to run it for me"** — by design (OQ1-redo). Run the
   195	  `!` line yourself; paste the output back if you want it read.
   196	- **"I ran `on` but the session still prompts"** — the toggle is
   197	  next-session; the running session keeps its boot posture. Start a
   198	  new session.
   199	- **Marker and settings disagree** (e.g. crash between the two writes,
   200	  or the overlay was hand-edited): `status` reports the disagreement
   201	  instead of picking a side. If the marker is **gone** but the overlay is
   202	  still armed, plain `off` is a no-op — run
   203	  `off --discard-snapshot` to strip the override, then `on` again if you
   204	  still want night mode. Never "fix" it by running `off` then `on` in
   205	  that state: `on` would snapshot the armed value.
   206	- **`off` refuses with "not a healthy night-mode marker"** — the marker
   207	  is gitignored, unguarded state and something outside night-mode wrote
   208	  it (a tampered `prev_value` such as `bypassPermissions` or
   209	  `acceptEdits`, a non-boolean field, a wrong `version`, or an
   210	  inconsistent `created_file`/`prev_present` pair). Validation is
   211	  fail-CLOSED: the overlay is untouched and the marker is kept as
   212	  evidence. Recover with:
   213	
   214	  ```
   215	  ! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py off --discard-snapshot
   216	  ```
   217	
   218	  This removes the override and the marker without honoring the
   219	  snapshot, and prints what it discarded. Keep a copy of the marker
   220	  first if you want to investigate who wrote it — the PostToolUse audit
   221	  log records tool writes to it.
   222	- **Non-zero exit with a parse diagnostic** — your existing
   223	  `settings.local.json` is malformed JSON. The script will not rewrite
   224	  or "repair" it (fail-closed on input). Fix the JSON by hand, re-run.
   225	- **Refused under CI** — expected: night-mode is operator/local only.
   226	- **What actually applies?** — trust `status`'s layer provenance line;
   227	  it uses the official resolver, not a re-implementation.
   228	
   229	## Exit codes
   230	
   231	- 0 — success (including idempotent no-ops)
   232	- non-zero — refused (CI set), malformed input, tampered/invalid marker,
   233	  or read-back mismatch; the settings file and marker are left in a
   234	  diagnosable state. Any refusal is recoverable with
   235	  `off --discard-snapshot` — no refusal can strand the posture armed.
