     8	`disableAutoMode: "disable"`) is NEVER touched — settings are read at
     9	session start, so the toggle takes effect on the NEXT session
    10	("arm before sleep, disarm in the morning" semantics, PLAN-165 fact #5).
    11	
    12	## Design decisions carried in from the PLAN-165 v2 review
    13	
    14	- **D1 — no `bypassPermissions`, anywhere.** The only mode `on` ever
    15	  writes is `acceptEdits`, and `off` restores only values from the
    16	  closed set `_RESTORABLE_MODES` — which NEVER includes
    17	  `bypassPermissions` (NM-01). `bypassPermissions` in ANY settings layer
    18	  trips `TAMPER_PERMISSION_BYPASS` (`_lib/effective_config.py`) and turns
    19	  `/ceo-boot` red by design; the honest escape valve is an explicit,
    20	  ephemeral `claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions` session.
    21	- **D2 — single-writer via ceremony prerequisite P1.** A deny rule for
    22	  Edit/Write on `.claude/settings.local.json` (sentinel ceremony, blocks
    23	  W1 landing) makes this script — a process write, not a tool write —
    24	  the only writer of the overlay. This script does not and cannot
    25	  enforce P1 itself; it relies on it for the escalation-ladder closure.
    26	- **D3 — the boot banner derives from the resolver, not the marker.**
    27	  The marker/snapshot file is decoration + restore-state only. `status`
    28	  reconciles marker vs `_lib.effective_config.resolve_settings()` and
    29	  REPORTS disagreement instead of picking a side.
    30	
    31	## State model (W0 decisions — fixed, do not revisit)
    32	
    33	Marker AND snapshot live in ONE file, `<project>/.claude/state/night-mode.json`
    34	(gitignored via the `.claude/state/` entry), same filesystem as the
    35	settings overlay (atomicity; kills the slug-convention ambiguity, W0 T0.7):
    36	
    37	    {"version": 1, "mode_written": "acceptEdits", "prev_present": bool,
    38	     "prev_value": null | <mode in _RESTORABLE_MODES>,
    39	     "created_file": bool, "ts": "...Z", "hostname": "..."}
    40	
    41	The file is gitignored, unguarded and agent-writable, so on the way IN
    42	(`off`) it is UNTRUSTED input: `_validate_marker` checks the whole
    43	document against that schema — including the consistency rule
    44	`created_file=True implies prev_present=False` — before any field is
    45	acted on (NF-03).
    46	
    47	The snapshot is CREATE-ONLY (contract item 4): if the marker exists, `on`
    48	is a no-op (exit 0). Without this, a second `on` would snapshot the value
    49	night-mode itself wrote and `off` would "restore" `acceptEdits` — a
    50	permanently weakened posture.
    51	
    52	## Write contract (PLAN-165 §Contrato de escrita — all items mandatory)
    53	
    54	1. `_lib.filelock.FileLock` around every whole mutation sequence.
    55	2. Atomic writes only: tempfile in the SAME directory, flush + fsync,
    56	   `os.replace`. Never a truncating in-place write — a torn/truncated
    57	   settings file makes the harness skip the WHOLE file (S286 class).
    58	3. Read-back after replace: reopen + re-parse; mismatch exits non-zero
    59	   WITHOUT touching the marker.
    60	4. Snapshot is create-only (above).
    61	5. Defined ordering: `on` writes settings THEN marker; `off` restores
    62	   settings THEN removes the marker. `status` reconciles and reports.
    63	6. Malformed existing input is fail-CLOSED (exit 2, no write, clear
    64	   diagnostic) — repo doctrine: fail-open on infra, fail-closed on input.
    65	7. Double-`on` / double-`off` are no-ops that exit 0.
    66	
    67	## Non-goals with enforcement
    68	
    69	No autonomy in CI: `on`/`off` REFUSE (exit 2) whenever the `CI` env var
    70	is present (even empty — fail-closed on presence). `status` stays
    71	available read-only. No tty gate (W0 T0.6: the Bash rail has no tty;
    72	Owner presence is guaranteed by P1 + the CI refusal).
    73	
    74	## Audit (NM-05 / NF-07 — two records, one per terminating path)
    75	
    76	Every `on`/`off` invocation leaves TWO records on EVERY terminating
    77	path, written as a pair, never one without the other:
    78	
    79	1. ONE machine-readable summary line on stdout (stable key=value shape) —
    80	   the operator-facing record, unsigned and terminal-scoped:
    81	
    82	       night-mode-event mode=... previous_mode=... result=applied|noop|refused|failed
    83	
    84	2. ONE `night_mode_toggled` row on the HMAC-chained audit log, via
    85	   `_emit_audit` -> `_lib.audit_emit.emit_night_mode_toggled` — the
    86	   FORENSIC record PLAN-165 AC-7 requires. The overlay is written by a
    87	   PROCESS, not by a tool, so no PostToolUse observer covers it: without
    88	   this row a posture flip between sessions leaves no signed trace.
    89	
    90	The action is REGISTERED (`_KNOWN_ACTIONS`, typed wrapper, deny-by-default
    91	scrub branch, SPEC row — sentinel ceremony `610d9ec`), so the emit is
    92	live. Only the four allowlisted fields travel (`mode`, `previous_mode`,
    93	`result`, `hostname_hash`); never a path, never file content, never the
    94	raw hostname. The emit is best-effort and NEVER raises: observability
    95	must not be able to block or fail the toggle.
    96	
    97	`status` emits NEITHER record — it is read-only, not a toggle.
    98	
    99	## Round-2 security hardening (see PLAN-165/architect/round-2/)
   100	
   101	- NM-01/NM-10 — `off` restores `prev_value` only if it is a string in
   102	  the closed set `_RESTORABLE_MODES`; anything else (wrong type, unknown
   103	  string, `bypassPermissions`) is fail-CLOSED exit 2 with the marker
   104	  left in place. A tampered marker can no longer launder a value into
   105	  the overlay through the Owner's own `off`.
   106	- NM-02 — `on` never snapshots night-mode's own value: if the overlay
   107	  already carries `acceptEdits` with no marker (crash/desync end-state),
   108	  the snapshot is normalized to "no prior override" so `off` restores
   109	  the project posture instead of freezing the weak one.
   110	- NM-04 — `--project-root` is confined to this repository unless the
   111	  test-only `CEO_NIGHT_MODE_TEST_SEAM` env var is set; the target must
   112	  already contain `.claude/settings.json`.
   113	- NM-09 — best-effort parent-directory fsync after `os.replace` and
   114	  after overlay/marker unlink.
   115	
   116	## Round-3 security hardening (findings NF-01..NF-04)
   117	
   118	- NF-01 — `_RESTORABLE_MODES` excludes `NIGHT_MODE` itself. `cmd_on`'s
   119	  desync normalization guarantees a healthy marker never carries
   120	  night-mode's own value, so admitting it could only ever admit a
   121	  TAMPERED value — and honoring it re-armed `acceptEdits` while REMOVING
   122	  the marker, leaving the posture armed with every later `off` a forever
   123	  no-op. `cmd_off` applies the same normalization symmetrically as a
   124	  second layer.
   125	- NF-02 — the restorable set is DERIVED from
   126	  `_HARNESS_PERMISSION_MODES`, the CLI's real `--permission-mode` enum,
   127	  instead of hand-listed: the hand-listed set carried a mode the harness
   128	  does not have (`default`) and omitted one it does (`dontAsk`), so a
   129	  legitimate `dontAsk` overlay was snapshotted by `on` and then refused
   130	  by `off` FOREVER with `acceptEdits` left armed — fail-closed landing on
   131	  the permissive side. `off --discard-snapshot` is the documented,
   132	  non-restoring recovery from every fail-closed refusal, referenced in
   133	  every refusal diagnostic.
   134	- NF-03 — `_validate_marker` validates the marker as a WHOLE DOCUMENT
   135	  before ANY field is acted on (version, mode_written, both booleans,
   136	  prev_value, and the `created_file=True implies prev_present=False`
   137	  consistency rule). Validating `prev_value` alone left the overlay
   138	  `os.unlink` branch reachable from a planted `created_file` — silent
   139	  data loss on an exit-0 `result=applied` path.
   140	- NF-04 — the one-summary-per-terminating-path requirement (and the P2
   141	  audit emit that lands beside it) is asserted STRUCTURALLY by an `ast`
   142	  oracle in `tests/test_night_mode.py`, not just described in prose.
   143	
   144	## Round-4 security hardening (findings NF-05..NF-06)
   145	
   146	One class, two surfaces: values from the two gitignored documents
   147	(`settings.local.json`, `.claude/state/night-mode.json`) were echoed into
   148	LINE-ORIENTED records without collapsing line breaks, so whoever can write
   149	either file forges whole extra lines in records the Owner and parsers
   150	trust.
   151	
   152	- NF-05 — `cmd_on`'s success line interpolated `previous` (the overlay's
   153	  RAW `permissions.defaultMode`) with neither `_bounded_repr` nor
   154	  `_summary_token`, the only untrusted echo in the file that did. A `\n`
   155	  in that value made ONE `on` emit TWO `night-mode-event ` rows, the
   156	  forged one claiming `result=applied`; a 200k-char value emitted 200k
   157	  bytes. It now goes through `_bounded_repr` like every sibling.
   158	- NF-06 — `cmd_status` never ran `_validate_marker` and printed
   159	  `host=` / `ts=` raw, and `_validate_marker` never checked those two
   160	  fields at all. So a planted `hostname` injected arbitrary status lines
   161	  (a forged `reconciliation:` verdict landing BEFORE the true one), and
   162	  `status` reported "AGREE — night-mode ON" for the very marker `off`
   163	  refuses with exit 2 — the reporter blessing what the writer rejects.
   164	  Now: `ts`/`hostname` are type-, length- and control-char-checked as part
   165	  of the whole document (`_invalid_bounded_string`), `status` RUNS the
   166	  validator and renders "PRESENT but INVALID (<field>)" with a DISAGREE
   167	  verdict for a rejected marker, every untrusted echo is bounded, and the
   168	  report is emitted through `_one_line` so no future field can forge a
   169	  record.
   170	
   171	## Round-3 RE-REVIEW hardening (findings NF-07, NF-09)
   172	
   173	- NF-07 — the `night_mode_toggled` action was registered, SPEC'd and
   174	  documented in signed surfaces, with ZERO production callers: a
   175	  registered-and-empty action, and three published claims that were
   176	  false. `_emit_audit` is now the sibling of every `_summary` call (see
   177	  `## Audit` above), so the signed claim "emitted on EVERY terminating
   178	  path of on/off" is true of this build. The `ast` oracle pins the PAIR,
   179	  not just the summary — a later refactor that drops one of the two is a
   180	  test failure, not a silent regression.
   181	- NF-09 — `off` on the route where the overlay had ALREADY been removed
   182	  by hand wrote nothing, then reported `restored to '<snapshot>'` with
   183	  `result=applied` on both records. It now reports `absent` / `noop` and
   184	  says plainly that nothing was restored. The bug was harmless while the
   185	  record was only a stdout line; with NF-07 wired it would have put a
   186	  false restore into the HMAC chain, which is why the two fixes belong in
   187	  one commit.
   188	
   189	- NF-08b — the script refuses to run unless the file it was invoked as IS
   190	  `<repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`. Round 2 of the cross-vendor
   191	  review found that the Bash matcher guarding this toggle recognises
   192	  SPELLINGS, and an alias exists to have a different one: `ln -s
   193	  <repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py /tmp/nm && python3 /tmp/nm on`
   194	  armed the posture (measured, `result=applied`), because
   195	  `Path(__file__).resolve()` follows the link back into the repository.
   196	  Re-probing widened the class — a renamed copy inside the repo
   197	  (`.claude/scripts/nm2.py`) armed it too, and no rule about `ln` would
   198	  have caught that. The guard is here rather than in the hook because it
   199	  holds however the alias was created and even if the hook never runs;
   200	  the hook keeps the cheap half (denying `ln` on the toggle) as the
   201	  earlier signal. An ancestor directory being a symlink is NOT an alias
   202	  (`/tmp` -> `/private/tmp`), so ancestors are resolved separately.
   203	
   204	Exit codes:
   205	    0 — success or idempotent no-op
   206	    1 — read-back mismatch / post-write verification failure
   207	    2 — usage error, CI refusal, alias/relocated invocation (NF-08b),
   208	        root confinement (NM-04), tampered or malformed marker (NM-01 /
   209	        NF-01..NF-03 / NF-06), or malformed input (all fail-closed).
   210	        Recover an armed posture from any of these with
   211	        `off --discard-snapshot`.
   212	
   213	Stdlib-only; Python >= 3.9.
   214	"""
   215	
   216	from __future__ import annotations
   217	
   218	import argparse
   219	import copy
   220	import hashlib
