     1	# Cost of Operation
     2	
     3	> What does it cost to run a project under `ceo-orchestration`?
     4	> Spoiler: less than running an unstructured Claude Code session
     5	> for the same work, because per-role model dispatch puts the cheap
     6	> models on high-frequency fan-out and saves the expensive model for
     7	> orchestration + critical VETOs.
     8	
     9	## TL;DR — order of magnitude
    10	
    11	A typical session that produces one PR-ready change with a code
    12	review + security check + tests:
    13	
    14	| Configuration | Per session | Per 50 sessions/month | Per 200 sessions/month |
    15	|---------------|-------------|------------------------|-------------------------|
    16	| **All-Opus** baseline (`CEO_MULTIMODEL_ENABLE=0`) | ~$1.23 | ~$62 | ~$246 |
    17	| **Per-role dispatch** (genuine, all native) | ~$1.06 | ~$53 | ~$212 |
    18	| **+ Cache hit** (warm gate-1, ~30% multi-turn savings) | ~$0.75 | ~$37 | ~$148 |
    19	| **Mitigated rail default-on** (ADR-082, actual default) | ~$1.23 | ~$62 | ~$246 |
    20	
    21	Computed at **Opus 4.8 ($5/$25 per Mtok)** — the current default-CEO /
    22	VETO model. Note the "Mitigated rail default-on" row lands at **≈ the
    23	all-Opus baseline**: under ADR-082, 4 of the 5 canonical archetypes
    24	inherit the Opus CEO model, so the genuine per-role split (~$1.06) is
    25	only realised if you disable mitigation. Numbers are rough. Real usage
    26	varies by skill mix, plan complexity,
    27	and how many debates you trigger. Use the
    28	[`ceo-cost.py`](#measuring-cost) tool to measure your actual spend
    29	on your audit log instead of trusting these estimates.
    30	
    31	## ⚠ Mitigated dispatch — Opus rate inheritance disclosure (audit-v2 C3-P0-03)
    32	
    33	> **PLAN-044 audit-v2 Wave B disclosure.** The "Per-role dispatch"
    34	> row above assumes ADR-052 §Role-to-model distribution applies
    35	> uniformly. **It does not at v1.11.0.** The actual default cost is
    36	> closer to the new "Mitigated rail default-on" row.
    37	
    38	Per **ADR-082** (PLAN-061 default-on, accepted 2026-04-27), only
    39	**1 of the 5 canonical archetypes runs on the native rail** at
    40	default settings: `code-reviewer`. The other 4 (`qa-architect`,
    41	`performance-engineer`, `security-engineer`, `devops`) default
    42	to **mitigated dispatch** — the harness re-routes the Task call
    43	through `subagent_type=general-purpose` with the role's persona
    44	injected via `## SKILL CONTENT`.
    45	
    46	**Cost consequence:** the `general-purpose` sub-agent **inherits
    47	the CEO model**, which is **Opus 4.8 by default** (not the
    48	Sonnet/Haiku that ADR-052 maps these roles to). Every
    49	`qa-architect / performance-engineer / security-engineer / devops`
    50	spawn therefore bills at **Opus rates ($5/$25 per Mtok)**, not
    51	the **$3/$15 Sonnet rates** suggested by the ADR-052 policy table.
    52	
    53	A representative session that would cost ~$1.06 under genuine per-role
    54	dispatch lands at ~$1.23 under the v1.11.0 default because ~75% of the
    55	spawn fan-out routes through Opus by inheritance — and at Opus 4.8 the
    56	Opus/Sonnet gap is small ($5/$25 vs $3/$15), so the inheritance penalty
    57	is now modest (~16%, not the ~60% it was at Opus-4.7 rates).
    58	
    59	### Why ADR-082 ships this default
    60	
    61	The H4 rail anomaly (ADR-080) found that only `code-reviewer`
    62	spawns are reliable on the native rail. Other archetypes empirically
    63	exhibit higher fabrication rates on the native rail in some
    64	release-channel + model combinations. ADR-082 trades cost for
    65	correctness on those 4 archetypes by default.
    66	
    67	### Override — restore native rail to all archetypes
    68	
    69	```bash
    70	# Force native rail for ALL archetypes (full ADR-052 honored).
    71	# Cost reverts to ~$1.06/session; correctness reverts to whatever
    72	# the H4 rail anomaly produces in your release channel.
    73	export CEO_MITIGATION_DISABLE=1
    74	```
    75	
    76	Or per-spawn via the dispatcher CLI:
    77	
    78	```bash
    79	.claude/scripts/inject-agent-context.sh --dispatch=native qa-architect "<task>"
    80	```
    81	
    82	See `docs/CEO-MITIGATION-DISPATCH.md` for the full routing rule
    83	and ADR-082 for the empirical rationale.
    84	
    85	### What `code-reviewer` does
    86	
    87	`code-reviewer` runs natively + at **Opus 4.8 by policy** (ADR-052
    88	VETO floor), not by inheritance. The cost there is identical
    89	under either rail. Only the other 4 canonicals see the Opus-by-
    90	inheritance surprise.
    91	
    92	## Inputs to the cost model
    93	
    94	### Anthropic public pricing (2025-2026)
    95	
    96	| Model | Input ($/M tokens) | Output ($/M tokens) | vs Opus 4-8 |
    97	|-------|--------------------|----------------------|-------------|
    98	| `claude-fable-5` (current — Mythos-class flagship) | $10.00 | $50.00 | 2.0× |
    99	| `claude-opus-5` (current) | $5.00 | $25.00 | 1.0× |
   100	| `claude-sonnet-5` (current; intro $2/$10 until 2026-08-31, sticker $3/$15) | $2.00 | $10.00 | 0.4× |
   101	| `claude-haiku-4-5` (current) | $1.00 | $5.00 | 0.2× |
   102	| `claude-opus-4-8` (N-1, active) | $5.00 | $25.00 | 1.0× |
   103	| `claude-opus-4-7` (historical — retained for log replay) | $15.00 | $75.00 | 3.0× |
   104	| `claude-sonnet-4-6` (N-1, active) | $3.00 | $15.00 | 0.6× |
   105	| `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` (dated pin of haiku-4-5) | $1.00 | $5.00 | 0.2× |
   106	
   107	Gen-5 rows added 2026-08-09 (PLAN-169 W2.10 D7, mirroring `ceo-cost.py`).
   108	Live-confirmed 2026-05-29 from https://www.anthropic.com/api (Opus 4.8 = $5/$25, Sonnet 4.6 = $3/$15, Haiku 4.5 = $1/$5). The prior Haiku row ($0.25/$1.25) propagated a stale rate from ADR-052 §Cost magnitude and underpriced Haiku 4x. Adopters with their own contractual
   109	pricing should override the table via `CEO_PRICING_PATH=<json>` —
   110	the `_lib/adapters/live/_cost.py` resolver picks it up.
   111	
   112	### Per-role distribution (ADR-052 default)
   113	
   114	| Agent | Model | Why |
   115	|-------|-------|-----|
   116	| **CEO orchestrator** (you, the chat session) | Opus 4.8 | Long context, L3+ decisions, debate synthesis |
   117	| **code-reviewer** | Opus 4.8 | Merge VETO — false negative ships a bug |
   118	| **security-engineer** | Opus 4.8 | Auth/crypto VETO — missed attack surface = incident |
   119	| **qa-architect** | Sonnet 4.6 | Edge-case enumeration; bounded work; cost 0.6× vs Opus 4.8 |
   120	| **performance-engineer** | Sonnet 4.6 | Metric analysis; deterministic; cost 0.6× vs Opus 4.8 |
   121	| **devops** | Haiku 4.5 | Config edits + boilerplate; high-freq + low-novelty; cost ~5× cheaper than Opus 4.8 |
   122	
   123	Domain agents (e.g. fintech `financial-correctness-and-math`) inherit
   124	the CEO model unless the project's `team-personas.md` specifies a
   125	`model:` override.
   126	
   127	### Cache behavior
   128	
   129	The gate-1 boot ceremony (CLAUDE.md + PROTOCOL.md + team.md +
   130	frontend-team.md + ceo-orchestration SKILL.md) is **~44,786 tokens**.
   131	Anthropic prompt caching gives ~90% read discount for cached prefix
   132	on subsequent turns within the 5-minute TTL.
   133	
   134	| Scenario | Cost impact |
   135	|----------|-------------|
   136	| First turn of a session (cold) | Full price for gate-1 boot |
   137	| Subsequent turns within 5 min | ~90% savings on cached prefix (the gate-1 boot fits the cache window) |
   138	| Cache miss (TTL expired or gate-1 invalidated) | Full price again |
   139	
   140	**The cache discipline rule** (CLAUDE.md §0): never edit gate-1
   141	files mid-session. Each mid-session edit costs ~44,786 tokens
   142	re-charge on the next turn. See `docs/opus-4-7-operations.md` §2 for
   143	the full discipline.
   144	
   145	PLAN-020 Phase 6 measured **97.14% spawn-prompt savings** when
   146	using `## SKILL REFERENCE` (Format B) vs inline `## SKILL CONTENT`
   147	(Format A) on the canonical-5 archetypes. Replay benchmark fixture:
   148	`replay-fixtures/plan-019-wave-2a.jsonl`. The 97.14% is an upper
   149	bound; real workloads include ~10ms sub-agent Read cost + ~50
   150	protocol tokens, so attribute "≥ 75% savings" in production.

